2026-06-20 22:00:09,015 - INFO - main.py:197 - FRONTEND DEBUG: {"level": "log", "message": "fetchQuestions: Received response data: {\"total_count\":5,\"questions\":[{\"question_id\":1536,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-06-15\",\"question_text\":\"An astronaut in space pushes off a wall of a spaceship. The astronaut has a mass of \\\\(70\\\\text{ kg}\\\\) and the spaceship has a mass of \\\\(7000\\\\text{ kg}\\\\). If the astronaut experiences an acceleration of \\\\(0.5\\\\text{ m/s}^2\\\\) in one direction, what is the magnitude of the acceleration the spaceship experiences in the opposite direction?\",\"choice_1\":\"\\\\(0.005\\\\text{ m/s}^2\\\\)\",\"choice_2\":\"\\\\(0.05\\\\text{ m/s}^2\\\\)\",\"choice_3\":\"\\\\(0.5\\\\text{ m/s}^2\\\\)\",\"choice_4\":\"\\\\(5\\\\text{ m/s}^2\\\\)\",\"correct_option\":\"A\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Medium\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Applying\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Communication Proficiency\",\"question_type\":\"multiple choice\",\"school_type\":\"International\",\"subject\":\"Science\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"According to Newton's Third Law, the force exerted by the astronaut on the spaceship is equal in magnitude and opposite in direction to the force exerted by the spaceship on the astronaut. Let this force be \\\\(F\\\\).\\n\\nFor the astronaut:\\n\\\\(F = m_{\\\\text{astronaut}} \\\\times a_{\\\\text{astronaut}}\\\\) \\n\\\\(F = 70\\\\text{ kg} \\\\times 0.5\\\\text{ m/s}^2 = 35\\\\text{ N}\\\\)\\n\\nFor the spaceship, the force acting on it is also \\\\(35\\\\text{ N}\\\\) (in the opposite direction).\\n\\nUsing Newton's Second Law for the spaceship:\\n\\\\(F = m_{\\\\text{spaceship}} \\\\times a_{\\\\text{spaceship}}\\\\) \\n\\\\(35\\\\text{ N} = 7000\\\\text{ kg} \\\\times a_{\\\\text{spaceship}}\\\\) \\n\\\\(a_{\\\\text{spaceship}} = 35\\\\text{ N} / 7000\\\\text{ kg} = 0.005\\\\text{ m/s}^2\\\\)\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1535,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-06-15\",\"question_text\":\"A block of mass \\\\(m\\\\) is placed on a frictionless horizontal surface. If a horizontal force \\\\(F\\\\) is applied to the block, causing an acceleration \\\\(a\\\\), what would be the acceleration if the force were doubled to \\\\(2F\\\\) and the mass were also doubled to \\\\(2m\\\\)?\",\"choice_1\":\"\\\\(a/2\\\\)\",\"choice_2\":\"\\\\(a\\\\)\",\"choice_3\":\"\\\\(2a\\\\)\",\"choice_4\":\"\\\\(4a\\\\)\",\"correct_option\":\"B\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Medium\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Applying\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Communication Proficiency\",\"question_type\":\"multiple choice\",\"school_type\":\"International\",\"subject\":\"Science\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"According to Newton's Second Law, the initial acceleration is given by \\\\(a = F/m\\\\). \\n\\nIn the new scenario, the force is doubled to \\\\(F' = 2F\\\\) and the mass is doubled to \\\\(m' = 2m\\\\). \\n\\nThe new acceleration \\\\(a'\\\\) would be:\\\\(a' = F'/m' = (2F) / (2m)\\\\). \\n\\nSimplifying the expression, we get:\\\\(a' = F/m\\\\). \\n\\nSince \\\\(F/m\\\\) is equal to the original acceleration \\\\(a\\\\), the new acceleration \\\\(a'\\\\) is still \\\\(a\\\\).\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1534,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-06-15\",\"question_text\":\"When a swimmer pushes water backward with their hands, they move forward. Which of Newton's Laws best explains this phenomenon?\",\"choice_1\":\"Newton's First Law of Motion\",\"choice_2\":\"Newton's Second Law of Motion\",\"choice_3\":\"Newton's Third Law of Motion\",\"choice_4\":\"Law of Conservation of Energy\",\"correct_option\":\"C\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Medium\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Applying\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Communication Proficiency\",\"question_type\":\"multiple choice\",\"school_type\":\"International\",\"subject\":\"Science\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"This phenomenon is an excellent example of Newton's Third Law of Motion, which states that for every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction. The action force is the swimmer pushing the water backward. The reaction force is the water pushing the swimmer forward with an equal magnitude, propelling the swimmer through the water.\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1533,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-06-15\",\"question_text\":\"A force of \\\\(20\\\\text{ N}\\\\) is applied to an object, causing it to accelerate at \\\\(4\\\\text{ m/s}^2\\\\). If the same force is applied to another object that has twice the mass, what will be the acceleration of the second object?\",\"choice_1\":\"\\\\(1\\\\text{ m/s}^2\\\\)\",\"choice_2\":\"\\\\(2\\\\text{ m/s}^2\\\\)\",\"choice_3\":\"\\\\(4\\\\text{ m/s}^2\\\\)\",\"choice_4\":\"\\\\(8\\\\text{ m/s}^2\\\\)\",\"correct_option\":\"B\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Medium\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Applying\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Communication Proficiency\",\"question_type\":\"multiple choice\",\"school_type\":\"International\",\"subject\":\"Science\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"According to Newton's Second Law, \\\\(F = ma\\\\). \\n\\nFirst, find the mass of the first object:\\\\(m_1 = F / a_1 = 20\\\\text{ N} / 4\\\\text{ m/s}^2 = 5\\\\text{ kg}\\\\). \\n\\nThe second object has twice the mass, so \\\\(m_2 = 2 \\\\times m_1 = 2 \\\\times 5\\\\text{ kg} = 10\\\\text{ kg}\\\\). \\n\\nNow, apply the same force \\\\(F = 20\\\\text{ N}\\\\) to the second object to find its acceleration \\\\(a_2\\\\):\\\\(a_2 = F / m_2 = 20\\\\text{ N} / 10\\\\text{ kg} = 2\\\\text{ m/s}^2\\\\).\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1532,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-06-15\",\"question_text\":\"A spaceship is traveling through space far from any gravitational influence. If its engines suddenly shut down, what will happen to the spaceship's motion according to Newton's First Law?\",\"choice_1\":\"It will immediately stop.\",\"choice_2\":\"It will gradually slow down and then stop.\",\"choice_3\":\"It will continue to move at a constant velocity.\",\"choice_4\":\"It will change direction.\",\"correct_option\":\"C\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Medium\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Applying\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Communication Proficiency\",\"question_type\":\"multiple choice\",\"school_type\":\"International\",\"subject\":\"Science\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"Newton's First Law (the Law of Inertia) states that an object in motion will stay in motion with the same speed and in the same direction unless acted upon by an unbalanced external force. In the vacuum of space, far from any gravitational fields or atmospheric resistance, there are no significant external forces to slow down or change the direction of the spaceship. Therefore, it will continue to move at a constant velocity.\",\"answers_hidden\":false}]}", "url": "http://localhost:6016/index.html", "timestamp": "2026-06-20T19:00:08.988Z"}
