2026-07-01 18:37:08,978 - INFO - main.py:197 - FRONTEND DEBUG: {"level": "log", "message": "fetchQuestions: Received response data: {\"total_count\":30,\"questions\":[{\"question_id\":1546,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-07-01\",\"question_text\":\"Which city currently serves as the capital of Algeria, a North African country with a rich history?\",\"choice_1\":\"Oran\",\"choice_2\":\"Algiers\",\"choice_3\":\"Constantine\",\"choice_4\":\"Tlemcen\",\"correct_option\":\"B\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Easy\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Applying\",\"learning_outcome\":\"ability to identify places\",\"question_type\":\"multiple choice\",\"school_type\":\"Secondary\",\"subject\":\"History\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"The capital and largest city of Algeria is Algiers. It is located on the Mediterranean Sea and is the country's chief port and industrial center. The other cities listed are important Algerian cities but not the capital.\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1541,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-06-16\",\"question_text\":\"Which of the following statements correctly describes Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL)?\",\"choice_1\":\"The sum of all voltages around any closed loop in a circuit is equal to zero.\",\"choice_2\":\"The algebraic sum of currents entering a junction (node) is equal to the algebraic sum of currents leaving that junction.\",\"choice_3\":\"The total resistance of a series circuit is the sum of individual resistances.\",\"choice_4\":\"The current flowing through a conductor between two points is directly proportional to the voltage across the two points.\",\"correct_option\":\"B\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Easy\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Remembering\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Communication Proficiency\",\"question_type\":\"multiple choice\",\"school_type\":\"Primary\",\"subject\":\"Science\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL) states that the algebraic sum of currents entering a junction (node) is equal to the algebraic sum of currents leaving that junction. This principle is based on the conservation of electric charge. Option A describes Kirchhoff's Voltage Law (KVL). Option C describes a property of series circuits, and Option D describes Ohm's Law.\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1540,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-06-16\",\"question_text\":\"[Dummy] Q1: Based on: dadc...\",\"choice_1\":\"1A\",\"choice_2\":\"1B\",\"choice_3\":\"1C\",\"choice_4\":\"1D\",\"correct_option\":\"A\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Medium\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Remembering\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Technical  Competence\",\"question_type\":\"multiple choice\",\"school_type\":\"Secondary\",\"subject\":\"Mathmatics\",\"year\":\"Year 4\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"This is a dummy step-by-step solution.\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1539,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-06-15\",\"question_text\":\"A spacecraft is moving through deep space at a constant velocity of \\\\(10,000\\\\) m/s relative to a fixed star. If its engines are turned off, what can be inferred about the net external force acting on the spacecraft?\",\"choice_1\":\"The net external force is directed opposite to the spacecraft's motion, causing it to slow down gradually.\",\"choice_2\":\"The net external force is zero, allowing the spacecraft to continue its motion at a constant velocity according to Newton's First Law.\",\"choice_3\":\"The net external force is in the direction of the spacecraft's motion, maintaining its high speed.\",\"choice_4\":\"The net external force is constant and non-zero, as there is still gravitational pull from distant celestial bodies.\",\"correct_option\":\"B\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Medium\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Applying\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Communication Proficiency\",\"question_type\":\"multiple choice\",\"school_type\":\"International\",\"subject\":\"Science\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"According to Newton's First Law of Motion, an object in motion will stay in motion with the same speed and in the same direction unless acted upon by an unbalanced external force. Since the spacecraft is moving at a constant velocity (meaning zero acceleration) and its engines are off (implying no thrust), the net external force acting on it must be zero. Even in deep space, there might be minuscule gravitational forces, but for practical applications and the context of 'constant velocity' without engines, the net force is considered negligible or zero, allowing the constant velocity to persist. Therefore, option B is the correct inference.\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1538,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-06-15\",\"question_text\":\"The integral equation \\\\(y(x) = f(x) + \\\\lambda \\\\int_{a}^{b} K(x,t) y(t) dt\\\\), where \\\\(a\\\\) and \\\\(b\\\\) are constants, is classified as a Volterra integral equation of the second kind.\",\"choice_1\":\"Accept\",\"choice_2\":\"Reject\",\"choice_3\":null,\"choice_4\":null,\"correct_option\":\"B\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Medium\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Evaluating\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Technical  Competence\",\"question_type\":\"accept_reject\",\"school_type\":\"International\",\"subject\":\"Mathmatics\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"1. An integral equation of the form \\\\(y(x) = f(x) + \\\\lambda \\\\int_{a}^{b} K(x,t) y(t) dt\\\\), where the upper limit of integration \\\\(b\\\\) is a constant, is known as a Fredholm integral equation of the second kind.\\n2. A Volterra integral equation of the second kind has the form \\\\(y(x) = f(x) + \\\\lambda \\\\int_{a}^{x} K(x,t) y(t) dt\\\\), where the upper limit of integration is the variable \\\\(x\\\\).\\n3. Since the given equation has a constant upper limit \\\\(b\\\\), it is a Fredholm integral equation, not a Volterra integral equation. Therefore, the statement incorrectly classifies the equation and should be rejected.\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1537,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-06-15\",\"question_text\":\"Consider the integral equation \\\\(y(x) = 1 + \\\\int_{0}^{x} y(t) dt\\\\). Is the function \\\\(y(x) = e^x\\\\) a solution to this equation?\",\"choice_1\":\"Accept\",\"choice_2\":\"Reject\",\"choice_3\":null,\"choice_4\":null,\"correct_option\":\"A\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Medium\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Evaluating\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Technical  Competence\",\"question_type\":\"accept_reject\",\"school_type\":\"International\",\"subject\":\"Mathmatics\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"To verify if \\\\(y(x) = e^x\\\\) is a solution, we substitute it into the integral equation:\\n1. Substitute \\\\(y(x) = e^x\\\\) into the integral equation: \\\\(e^x = 1 + \\\\int_{0}^{x} e^t dt\\\\).\\n2. Evaluate the definite integral: \\\\(\\\\int_{0}^{x} e^t dt = [e^t]_{0}^{x} = e^x - e^0 = e^x - 1\\\\).\\n3. Substitute the result of the integral back into the equation: \\\\(e^x = 1 + (e^x - 1)\\\\).\\n4. Simplify the right side: \\\\(e^x = 1 + e^x - 1\\\\), which simplifies to \\\\(e^x = e^x\\\\).\\nSince the equality holds true for all \\\\(x\\\\), the function \\\\(y(x) = e^x\\\\) is indeed a solution to the given integral equation. Therefore, the statement should be accepted.\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1521,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-05-15\",\"question_text\":\"\u0647\u0644 \u064a\u0646\u0635 \u0642\u0627\u0646\u0648\u0646 \u0627\u0644\u062c\u0627\u0630\u0628\u064a\u0629 \u0639\u0644\u0649 \u0623\u0646 \u0643\u0644 \u062c\u0633\u0645\u064a\u0646 \u0641\u064a \u0627\u0644\u0643\u0648\u0646 \u064a\u062c\u0630\u0628\u0627\u0646 \u0628\u0639\u0636\u0647\u0645\u0627 \u0627\u0644\u0628\u0639\u0636 \u0628\u0642\u0648\u0629\u061f\",\"choice_1\":\"\u0646\u0639\u0645\",\"choice_2\":\"\u0644\u0627\",\"choice_3\":null,\"choice_4\":null,\"correct_option\":\"A\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Easy\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Remembering\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Communication Proficiency\",\"question_type\":\"yes_no\",\"school_type\":\"International\",\"subject\":\"Science\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"\u064a\u0646\u0635 \u0642\u0627\u0646\u0648\u0646 \u0627\u0644\u062c\u0627\u0630\u0628\u064a\u0629 \u0627\u0644\u0643\u0648\u0646\u064a\u0629 \u0644\u0646\u064a\u0648\u062a\u0646 \u0639\u0644\u0649 \u0623\u0646 \u0643\u0644 \u062c\u0633\u0645\u064a\u0646 \u0641\u064a \u0627\u0644\u0643\u0648\u0646 \u064a\u062c\u0630\u0628\u0627\u0646 \u0628\u0639\u0636\u0647\u0645\u0627 \u0627\u0644\u0628\u0639\u0636 \u0628\u0642\u0648\u0629 \u062a\u062a\u0646\u0627\u0633\u0628 \u0637\u0631\u062f\u064a\u064b\u0627 \u0645\u0639 \u062d\u0627\u0635\u0644 \u0636\u0631\u0628 \u0643\u062a\u0644\u062a\u064a\u0647\u0645\u0627 \u0648\u0639\u0643\u0633\u064a\u064b\u0627 \u0645\u0639 \u0645\u0631\u0628\u0639 \u0627\u0644\u0645\u0633\u0627\u0641\u0629 \u0628\u064a\u0646 \u0645\u0631\u0643\u0632\u064a\u0647\u0645\u0627. \u0644\u0630\u0644\u0643\u060c \u0627\u0644\u0625\u062c\u0627\u0628\u0629 \u0647\u064a \u0646\u0639\u0645.\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1482,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-05-04\",\"question_text\":\"Which of the following statements correctly describe Kirchhoff's Laws?\",\"choice_1\":\"A. Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL) states that the algebraic sum of currents entering a junction is equal to the algebraic sum of currents leaving it.\",\"choice_2\":\"B. Kirchhoff's Voltage Law (KVL) states that the algebraic sum of potential differences (voltages) in any closed loop is equal to zero.\",\"choice_3\":\"C. Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL) is fundamentally based on the principle of conservation of energy.\",\"choice_4\":\"D. Kirchhoff's Voltage Law (KVL) is fundamentally based on the principle of conservation of charge.\",\"correct_option\":\"AB\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Easy\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Understanding\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Communication Proficiency\",\"question_type\":\"multiple_answer\",\"school_type\":\"International\",\"subject\":\"Science\",\"year\":\"Year 11\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL), also known as Kirchhoff's first law, states that the algebraic sum of currents entering a junction (or node) is equal to the algebraic sum of currents leaving it. This law is a direct consequence of the conservation of electric charge. So, statement A is correct.\\nKirchhoff's Voltage Law (KVL), also known as Kirchhoff's second law, states that the algebraic sum of the potential differences (voltages) around any closed loop in a circuit is equal to zero. This law is a direct consequence of the conservation of energy. So, statement B is correct.\\nStatement C is incorrect because KCL is based on the conservation of charge, not energy. Statement D is incorrect because KVL is based on the conservation of energy, not charge.\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1481,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-05-04\",\"question_text\":\"Does Newton's First Law of Motion, also known as the Law of Inertia, state that an object at rest will remain at rest, and an object in motion will stay in motion with the same speed and in the same direction, unless acted upon by an unbalanced external force?\",\"choice_1\":\"Yes\",\"choice_2\":\"No\",\"choice_3\":null,\"choice_4\":null,\"correct_option\":\"A\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Easy\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Understanding\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Technical Competence\",\"question_type\":\"yes_no\",\"school_type\":\"Secondary\",\"subject\":\"Physics\",\"year\":\"Year 11\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"Newton's First Law of Motion accurately describes that an object's state of motion (either at rest or moving at a constant velocity) will only change if an external net force acts upon it. This principle is fundamental to understanding inertia.\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1480,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-05-04\",\"question_text\":\"[Dummy] Q1: Based on: newton laws...\",\"choice_1\":\"Yes\",\"choice_2\":\"No\",\"choice_3\":null,\"choice_4\":null,\"correct_option\":\"A\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Easy\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Understanding\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Technical Competence\",\"question_type\":\"yes_no\",\"school_type\":\"Secondary\",\"subject\":\"Physics\",\"year\":\"Year 11\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"This is a dummy step-by-step solution.\",\"answers_hidden\":false}]}", "url": "http://localhost:6016/index.html", "timestamp": "2026-07-01T15:37:08.974Z"}
