2026-07-06 14:13:55,591 - INFO - main.py:197 - FRONTEND DEBUG: {"level": "log", "message": "fetchQuestions: Received response data: {\"total_count\":1541,\"questions\":[{\"question_id\":1548,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-07-06\",\"question_text\":\"Which of Newton's laws describes the tendency of an object to resist changes in its state of motion, often referred to as inertia?\",\"choice_1\":\"A. Newton's First Law of Motion\",\"choice_2\":\"B. Newton's Second Law of Motion\",\"choice_3\":\"C. Newton's Third Law of Motion\",\"choice_4\":\"D. Newton's Law of Universal Gravitation\",\"correct_option\":\"A\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Easy\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Understanding\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Communication Proficiency\",\"question_type\":null,\"school_type\":\"International\",\"subject\":\"Science\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"Newton's First Law of Motion, also known as the law of inertia, states that an object at rest stays at rest and an object in motion stays in motion with the same speed and in the same direction unless acted upon by an unbalanced force. This intrinsic property of matter to resist changes in its state of motion is called inertia, which is directly addressed by the first law. Newton's Second Law relates force, mass, and acceleration (\\\\(F=ma\\\\)), while Newton's Third Law describes action-reaction pairs. The Law of Universal Gravitation describes the attractive force between any two masses.\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1547,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-07-04\",\"question_text\":\"A rocket launches from Earth, accelerating upwards into space. Which of Newton's Laws primarily explains the origin of the propulsive force that causes the rocket to accelerate, and how does it relate to the expulsion of hot gases?\",\"choice_1\":\"A. Newton's First Law: The rocket overcomes its static inertia, and the gases are merely a byproduct of this process.\",\"choice_2\":\"B. Newton's Second Law: The net upward force on the rocket, caused by the expulsion of gases, directly results in its acceleration \\\\(F=ma\\\\).\",\"choice_3\":\"C. Newton's Third Law: The rocket expels hot gases downwards (action), and these gases exert an equal and opposite force upwards on the rocket (reaction), causing it to accelerate.\",\"choice_4\":\"D. Newton's Law of Universal Gravitation: The rocket's engines create a gravitational anomaly that repels it upwards, overcoming Earth's gravity.\",\"correct_option\":\"C\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Medium\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Applying\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Communication Proficiency\",\"question_type\":\"multiple choice\",\"school_type\":\"International\",\"subject\":\"Science\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"The propulsion of a rocket is a classic example of Newton's Third Law of Motion. Here's why:\\n\\n1.  **Newton's Third Law states:** For every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction.\\n2.  **Action:** The rocket engines burn fuel, expelling hot gases downwards with a certain force.\\n3.  **Reaction:** According to Newton's Third Law, these expelled gases exert an equal and opposite force upwards on the rocket itself.\\n4.  **Result:** This upward reaction force is the thrust that propels the rocket upwards, causing it to accelerate. While Newton's Second Law (\\\\(F=ma\\\\)) describes how this net force leads to acceleration, the *origin* of the propulsive force itself is explained by the action-reaction pair described by Newton's Third Law. Newton's First Law deals with inertia and the state of motion in the absence of net forces, and Newton's Law of Universal Gravitation describes the attractive force between masses, neither of which explains the propulsive mechanism.\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1546,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-07-01\",\"question_text\":\"Which city currently serves as the capital of Algeria, a North African country with a rich history?\",\"choice_1\":\"Oran\",\"choice_2\":\"Algiers\",\"choice_3\":\"Constantine\",\"choice_4\":\"Tlemcen\",\"correct_option\":\"B\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Easy\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Applying\",\"learning_outcome\":\"ability to identify places\",\"question_type\":\"multiple choice\",\"school_type\":\"Secondary\",\"subject\":\"History\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"The capital and largest city of Algeria is Algiers. It is located on the Mediterranean Sea and is the country's chief port and industrial center. The other cities listed are important Algerian cities but not the capital.\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1545,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-06-22\",\"question_text\":\"\u0625\u0630\u0627 \u0623\u062b\u0631\u062a \u0642\u0648\u0629 \u0639\u0644\u0649 \u062c\u0633\u0645 \u0643\u062a\u0644\u062a\u0647 \\\\(5 \\\\text{ kg}\\\\) \u0641\u0623\u0643\u0633\u0628\u062a\u0647 \u062a\u0633\u0627\u0631\u0639\u064b\u0627 \u0645\u0642\u062f\u0627\u0631\u0647 \\\\(2 \\\\text{ m/s}^2\\\\)\u060c \u0641\u0645\u0627 \u0645\u0642\u062f\u0627\u0631 \u0647\u0630\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0642\u0648\u0629\u061f\",\"choice_1\":\"\\\\(7 \\\\text{ N}\\\\)\",\"choice_2\":\"\\\\(10 \\\\text{ N}\\\\)\",\"choice_3\":\"\\\\(2.5 \\\\text{ N}\\\\)\",\"choice_4\":\"\\\\(0.4 \\\\text{ N}\\\\)\",\"correct_option\":\"B\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Easy\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Applying\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Communication Proficiency\",\"question_type\":null,\"school_type\":\"International\",\"subject\":\"Science\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"\u0648\u0641\u0642\u064b\u0627 \u0644\u0642\u0627\u0646\u0648\u0646 \u0646\u064a\u0648\u062a\u0646 \u0627\u0644\u062b\u0627\u0646\u064a \u0644\u0644\u062d\u0631\u0643\u0629\u060c \u0641\u0625\u0646 \u0627\u0644\u0642\u0648\u0629 (F) \u0627\u0644\u0645\u0624\u062b\u0631\u0629 \u0639\u0644\u0649 \u062c\u0633\u0645 \u062a\u0633\u0627\u0648\u064a \u062d\u0627\u0635\u0644 \u0636\u0631\u0628 \u0643\u062a\u0644\u062a\u0647 (m) \u0641\u064a \u062a\u0633\u0627\u0631\u0639\u0647 (a). \u064a\u0645\u0643\u0646 \u0627\u0644\u062a\u0639\u0628\u064a\u0631 \u0639\u0646 \u0630\u0644\u0643 \u0628\u0627\u0644\u0635\u064a\u063a\u0629: \\\\(F = m \\\\times a\\\\).\\n\u0628\u0627\u0644\u062a\u0639\u0648\u064a\u0636 \u0628\u0627\u0644\u0642\u064a\u0645 \u0627\u0644\u0645\u0639\u0637\u0627\u0629:\\n\u0627\u0644\u0643\u062a\u0644\u0629 \\\\(m = 5 \\\\text{ kg}\\\\)\\n\u0627\u0644\u062a\u0633\u0627\u0631\u0639 \\\\(a = 2 \\\\text{ m/s}^2\\\\)\\n\u0625\u0630\u0646\u060c \u0627\u0644\u0642\u0648\u0629 \\\\(F = 5 \\\\text{ kg} \\\\times 2 \\\\text{ m/s}^2 = 10 \\\\text{ N}\\\\).\\n\u0644\u0630\u0644\u0643\u060c \u0645\u0642\u062f\u0627\u0631 \u0627\u0644\u0642\u0648\u0629 \u0647\u0648 \\\\(10 \\\\text{ N}\\\\).\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1544,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-06-20\",\"question_text\":\"\u0623\u064a \u0646\u0647\u0631 \u0645\